8. auto vs static — Key Difference
auto variable

When a function is started, auto variable is created inside the function body.
As soon as the function is completed, auto variable is destroyed.
Purpose: when we want to maintain an individual copy inside every function call → use auto.

static variable

In a program, if there is a static variable:

It is created only for the first time.
From the next time onwards, new memory is NOT created.
The previously created memory is updated.




Example — static counter
cvoid test() {
    int l = 0;          // auto
    static int s;       // static
    ++s; ++l;
    printf("%d %d\n", l, s);
}

int main() {
    test();
    test();
    test();
    test();
    return 0;
}
Output:
1  1
1  2
1  3
Explanation:
Call 1: l created=0, s created=0 → l=1, s=1 → print 1 1
Call 2: l created=0(new), s=1(same) → l=1, s=2 → print 1 2
Call 3: l created=0(new), s=2(same) → l=1, s=3 → print 1 3

Bank Account Example (static use case)
